## K-r15.1-DF — the Dickson `x + 1/x` fibration (scope: this lens only)

**Killed** r15.1, 2026-09-13. **Seat** soulbis (Opus) at probe 5; critic: structural.

**The claim.** Replace the power map by the pair-closed Dickson fibration at h=512 with J=6
pins plus the palindromic double root at `u = 1`, hoping the square-class discount `(p−1)/2`
buys the 2.0035 bits at 121857.

**The kill.** `W_U(0) = 1` identically (every factor has constant term 1): no cheap group
0-root at all. The only sub-`p` pin is the double root at `u = 1` in the coset `2·Sq` of size
`(p−1)/2`; count `floor(C(255,136)/(p^6·(p−1)/2)) = 16479333838`, **23.99 bits short**
(24.99 at the standard rate; only a root charged nothing would clear, +5.9965). Lens scan over
`h ∈ {128..8192}`, both singleton and core conventions, every selection size, every index in
`(80811, 122362]`: nothing clears, best −24.8991. Small analogue `q = 193/769`: the only
confined Dickson pin is `w = 1` at image exactly `(q−1)/2`. The proposer's count was 0.91 bits
generous (`C(255,136)` for `C(254,135)`) — direction favours the kill. Universality ("only
`x^h` and Dickson descend") rests on Ritt, char 0, untrusted — **not** filed as proven.

**Re-open condition.** (a) a fibration of `μ_n` of degree `<= h` other than `x^h` and Dickson
with an exact char-`p` descent proof carrying a group root of image `<= H`; or (b) a pin on any
locator with image `<= (p−1)/8`, verified first on the `q = 193/769` analogue; or (c) a
two-fibre core buying the second rational root for free (D7; condition prices −0.91 / −2.91
bits, not total margins). Evidence: `runs/r15.1/r15.1.1/p2-dickson-fibration-priced-kill/`.

